Absolutely 👍 Since you’re learning **STL Pair in C++**, it’s useful to compare how the same concept works in **C++, Java, and Python**.

## 🔹 Pair: C++ vs Java vs Python

| Feature              | C++           | Java                                | Python                     |
| -------------------- | ------------- | ----------------------------------- | -------------------------- |
| Built-in pair?       | ✅ `pair`      | ❌ No direct `Pair` in standard Java | ❌ No built-in `pair`       |
| Common alternative   | `pair<T1,T2>` | `Map.Entry`, custom class, `record` | `tuple`                    |
| Access first value   | `.first`      | `.getKey()` / custom field          | `[0]`                      |
| Access second value  | `.second`     | `.getValue()` / custom field        | `[1]`                      |
| Different data types | ✅ Yes         | ✅ Yes                               | ✅ Yes                      |
| Mutable              | ✅ Yes         | Depends on implementation           | Tuple ❌, list ✅            |
| Header/import        | `<utility>`   | `java.util` for some alternatives   | No import needed for tuple |

---

# 1. C++ — `pair`

C++ has a **direct built-in `pair`**.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <utility>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    pair<int, string> p = {101, "Ravi"};

    cout << p.first << endl;
    cout << p.second << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

### Output

```text
101
Ravi
```

### Remember

```cpp
p.first
p.second
```

C++ is very convenient here.

---

# 2. Java — No direct standard `Pair`

Java does **not have a `Pair` class in the standard core collections** like C++ does.

You commonly use a custom class/`record`, or sometimes `Map.Entry`.

For example, using a `record`:

```java
record Pair(int first, String second) {}

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Pair p = new Pair(101, "Ravi");

        System.out.println(p.first());
        System.out.println(p.second());
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
101
Ravi
```

Notice the difference:

### C++

```cpp
p.first
p.second
```

### Java record

```java
p.first()
p.second()
```

Java uses **methods** to access record components.

---

# 3. Python — `tuple`

Python commonly uses a **tuple** when you want to store two related values.

```python
p = (101, "Ravi")

print(p[0])
print(p[1])
```

### Output

```text
101
Ravi
```

Here:

```text
p[0] → 101
p[1] → Ravi
```

Python also supports **unpacking**:

```python
p = (101, "Ravi")

id, name = p

print(id)
print(name)
```

This is one of the really nice things about Python. 😄

