# 🔹 Nested Pair Comparison

This is particularly important because you were learning:

```cpp
pair<int, pair<int,int>>
```

### C++

```cpp
pair<int, pair<int,int>> p = {1, {10, 20}};

cout << p.first << endl;
cout << p.second.first << endl;
cout << p.second.second << endl;
```

Output:

```text
1
10
20
```

### Python

A tuple can naturally do something similar:

```python
p = (1, (10, 20))

print(p[0])
print(p[1][0])
print(p[1][1])
```

Output:

```text
1
10
20
```

### Java

Java doesn't have the same simple nested-pair syntax. You'd normally create a class/record structure:

```java
record Pair(int first, int second) {}
record Data(int first, Pair second) {}
```

Then:

```java
Data p = new Data(1, new Pair(10, 20));

System.out.println(p.first());
System.out.println(p.second().first());
System.out.println(p.second().second());
```

---

# 🧠 Easy way to remember

Think of it like this:

```text
C++    → pair
Java   → record / custom class
Python → tuple
```

And accessing them:

```text
C++    → p.first, p.second
Java   → p.first(), p.second()
Python → p[0], p[1]
```

### One important point for your STL learning

In **C++**, `pair` is especially important because you'll see it heavily with:

```cpp
map<int, string>
```

and algorithms such as:

```cpp
sort()
```

and later with things like:

```cpp
vector<pair<int,int>>
```

So for your current STL journey, **`vector<pair<int,int>>` is definitely worth practicing next.**
